 ## Overview
-Cell-Projection Volume Rendering is an object-order volume rendering method for unstructured grids that projects mesh cells into image space to generate ray segments, which are then merged in image space [S1, S2, unofficial][S5]. Unlike ray-casting, it avoids the need for global depth-sorting of cells by having each processor scan-convert its local unstructured cells into ray segments, which are then routed to their final image destinations for merging [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from synchronous cell projection by allowing asynchronous communication and task management to hide data transfer overheads [S1, unofficial].
+Parallel Cell Projection is a distributed-memory object-order volume rendering algorithm for unstructured grids that projects mesh cells into image space to generate ray segments, which are then merged in image space [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S5]. Unlike ray-casting, it avoids the need for global depth-sorting of cells by having each processor scan-convert its local unstructured cells into ray segments, which are then routed to their final image destinations for merging [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from synchronous cell projection by allowing asynchronous communication and task management to hide data transfer overheads [S1, unofficial].
 
